WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.760
We have just eight days to&nbsp;
go until the 2020 election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.760
and the election is essentially a baked cake&nbsp;
at this point in time with just days remaining.&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.840
The two candidates couldn't be more different. The&nbsp;
number of undecideds is relatively small. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.440
the top issues to voters are corona virus,&nbsp;
the economy and the race related protests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.160
that we've seen over the last several months,&nbsp;
followed by health care and a number of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.760
different issues. And I do think it's important&nbsp;
just to remember that this is a unique election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.560
The circumstances are particularly different.&nbsp;
We're in the middle of a 100 year pandemic. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.400
got the most incompetent president that we've ever&nbsp;
had and that there are really important issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.960
that neither candidate is talking about&nbsp;
and that are rarely talked about in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:56.640
presidential elections. But we need to remember&nbsp;
this large swath of important issues that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.640
Joe Biden were to win, we are going to need to&nbsp;
get right back to shining a spotlight on. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.440
the point of doing this segment is not to attack&nbsp;
either candidate, per say. It is a reminder about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.200
circumstances. This is not about who we should&nbsp;
vote for. Obviously, for the sake of humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.000
and the future of the country, we must vote&nbsp;
for Joe Biden. But all of that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:28.720
there has been no substantive discussion about as&nbsp;
an example. Labor unions and the democratization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.320
of the workplace. There has been no substantive&nbsp;
discussion about tech monopolies outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.960
Donald Trump complaining about how Twitter and&nbsp;
Google treat him unfairly. No discussion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.720
massive change to our economy that has happened&nbsp;
and is happening, which means quite simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.240
that a ton of those manufacturing jobs are never&nbsp;
coming back because they don't exist. These aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.880
trivial things and they go to the core of how&nbsp;
our country has changed and potentially could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.840
change depending on who we elect. And the&nbsp;
reason it's crucial that we talk about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.560
is that someday the pandemic will be over.&nbsp;
Someday, hopefully, there will be a reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.800
person in the Oval Office again. Hopefully the&nbsp;
latter will happen in January. And we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:24.000
to need to be ready to get back to the serious&nbsp;
work on issues that are sort of higher on Maslow's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.160
hierarchy of needs for lack of a better term.&nbsp;
I'll get back to that idea. But let's go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.280
the issues. Back in 2016, I talked about&nbsp;
this and it's happening again, which is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.680
neither candidate will just come out and say a lot&nbsp;
of the manufacturing jobs are never coming back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.160
Many of the jobs just don't exist anymore&nbsp;
because they've been replaced by technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.200
Others aren't going to come back unless we change&nbsp;
our economy so drastically that labor is as cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.040
here as it is in Bangladesh and China and other&nbsp;
countries. And of course, our economy is based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:04.000
the labor being cheaper there than here. So if we&nbsp;
don't reorganize the economy and keep pretending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.720
that it's just a battle over who can bring those&nbsp;
jobs back, we're in a fantasy world that has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.600
dealt with. Neither Trump nor Hillary would say it&nbsp;
in 2016. They would both say, I'll bring the jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.800
back. No, I'll bring the jobs back, because it's&nbsp;
not politically advantageous to admit many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.680
jobs aren't coming back no matter who we elect. If&nbsp;
you say it, your opponent says that's a defeatist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:30.240
attitude and it's happening again this election&nbsp;
until we recognize and admit that we won't move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.920
in terms of figuring out, OK, given that many of&nbsp;
those jobs aren't coming back. How do we organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.440
structure and supplement our economy? Biden has&nbsp;
done a little bit of it by saying this isn't about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.800
saving jobs in a dying industry like coal. It's&nbsp;
about creating new industries altogether, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.560
green energy jobs. But both candidates are&nbsp;
very lacking on that issue. Labor unions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.800
democratizing the workplace. Neither candidate is&nbsp;
saying a word about it other than claiming I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.720
the support of the unions. Joe Biden has actually&nbsp;
been okay on this issue at times in his career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.520
Big Amtrak guy, which is a union represented&nbsp;
organization. But looking at the industries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.800
where workers are most vulnerable and figuring&nbsp;
out how do we increase union representation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.440
how do we change the fundamental dynamics of the&nbsp;
relationships between corporations and workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.880
to be more like the countries that don't need&nbsp;
as strong of a union presence because they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.160
dealt with the issues that unions are often&nbsp;
there to to deal with. Talking about employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.840
representation on company boards, dealing with&nbsp;
stock repurchasing, which just helps shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.800
and executives. If this all sounds weird, it's&nbsp;
because neither candidate talks about it for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.280
combination of reasons. Trump doesn't talk about&nbsp;
it because he doesn't support any of this stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.520
Biden, again, may support some of it. His&nbsp;
historical views on Amtrak suggests that he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.160
but he's not talking about it. Maybe because we&nbsp;
have a five or a five alarm fire when it comes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.120
Corona coronavirus in the economy. Maybe it's&nbsp;
because he's decided it's not to his advantage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.160
But either way, we have to get back to that&nbsp;
one. Sanity returns. And then number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.680
tech monopolies. Elizabeth Warren and Bernie&nbsp;
Sanders did talk about it. Andrew Yang talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.680
about it with regard to our data, but we're not&nbsp;
hearing about it from Trump nor Biden. Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.960
sort of will dance around it when he's mad&nbsp;
at tech companies, which he says are far left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.480
and are being unfair to him. It's&nbsp;
totally self-serving. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.880
actually being dealt with as an issue. And Joe&nbsp;
Biden's not really talking about it either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.240
In a prominent way. And by the way, the debate&nbsp;
moderators haven't brought up these issues either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.760
So we can say that there's a lot of blame to&nbsp;
go around here. So the point is not to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.960
that I have any doubt about who we should vote&nbsp;
for on Tuesday or or now if you're voting now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.560
It's obviously Joe Biden. And to some degree, we&nbsp;
have an emergency like Corona virus. And so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.640
natural that some of these issues would fall back.&nbsp;
If we go to Maslow's hierarchy of needs, if our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.760
basic needs of safety, security and health aren't&nbsp;
met. It's less likely we're going to get into tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.800
monopolies. And I understand that. But we must&nbsp;
remember that there are major political issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.080
missing from the campaign right now. We can say&nbsp;
they're missing because of coronavirus. We can say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.000
they're missing because Trump is such a dumpster&nbsp;
fire that until he's removed, we can't deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.480
with them. And that's fine. But we need a plan for&nbsp;
when we get the ball back. As I've been saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.280
Biden winning is getting the ball back. We're not&nbsp;
scoring points. We're just getting the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.480
to play offense again. We need to be ready to do&nbsp;
it, particularly since there may be a two year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.400
window of Senate and House control, which will&nbsp;
then be be replaced by Republicans taking control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:48.160
back of either or both the House and Senate.&nbsp;
So very important that we bear this in mind.

